{
	"$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
	"name": "sylvan-librarian",
	"main": "src/index.ts",
	"compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
	// No compatibility flags, and that is a MEASURED choice (2026-09-09, three
	// unpromoted `wrangler versions upload` per arm, interleaved, reading the
	// "Worker Startup Time" Cloudflare prints; see README, startup):
	//
	//   none                                  4 / 4 / 5 ms   <- the hello-world floor
	//   nodejs_compat  (what shipped before)  9 / 12 / 12 ms
	//   new_module_registry                  30 / 32 / 38 ms
	//   both                                 46 / 49 / 59 ms
	//
	// `nodejs_compat` bought nothing — the bundle has no `node:` import, no
	// `process`, no `Buffer` — and cost ~8ms of every cold isolate against the
	// free plan's 10ms-per-request CPU. TRAP: for compatibility dates >= 2026-08-04
	// Node compat is ON BY DEFAULT, so bumping the date turns it back on unless
	// BOTH `no_nodejs_compat` and `no_nodejs_compat_v2` are added here.
	//
	// `new_module_registry` (workerd's URL-based registry, opt-in) is a 3x startup
	// regression on this Worker: its lazy per-module compilation appears to bypass
	// the deploy-time compile that makes script size free here. Re-measure before
	// ever enabling it; do not assume a later workerd fixed it.
	"compatibility_flags": [],
	// No esbuild `keepNames`. Wrangler's default wraps every function and class in the bundle in a
	// `__name(fn, "fn")` call — 807 `Object.defineProperty` calls at module evaluation, in every
	// isolate: ~0.6ms of it (node 24, warm compile cache, 60 cold processes; `wrangler check
	// startup` 2.12 -> 1.45ms active). Nothing reads a function's `.name`: wire node types and
	// error names are explicit strings. Unminified, names survive anyway, except where esbuild
	// renames a colliding symbol (`decoder3`), which then shows in a stack trace under that name.
	"keep_names": false,
	// Console lines are logged in full (`head_sampling_rate` 1, the default), on purpose: the one
	// thing sampling would thin is the nightly coordinator's alarm chain, which is exactly the trace
	// a hang investigation needs. INVOCATION logs are off. Measured on DeckGen for 2026-09-21:
	// 1.25M events, of which 1.09M were the automatic one-record-per-call invocation logs (938k
	// Durable Object RPCs, 156k fetches) — ~37M/month against the paid plan's 20M included, and on
	// the free plan past the 200k/day cap by ~05:00 UTC, i.e. before the 11:17 nightly, with the rest
	// of the day dropped. Nothing here reads them: request counts, errors and CPU come from the
	// GraphQL analytics, and every investigation in this repo has read console lines. What is lost
	// is the per-request row in the dashboard's Logs view. Uncaught failures still reach the logs as
	// console.error lines from the Worker's handler and the coordinator's alarm body.
	"observability": { "enabled": true, "logs": { "invocation_logs": false } },

	// Static assets, served straight from Cloudflare's CDN out of public/ —
	// app.js, app.min.js, card.js, styles.css, the icons, robots.txt and the
	// tuner page. A request matching one of these NEVER invokes the Worker: no
	// isolate start, no CPU, and it does not count against the Worker request
	// budget. They also leave the script entirely, which is the point: they were
	// a ~313KB text module inside it, and script size costs isolate startup at
	// roughly 1.5ms per 100KB — a cost every /search was paying to carry files
	// it never reads (measured: 313KB 10-13ms, 0.5KB 7ms, empty Worker 5ms).
	//
	// DO NOT EXTRAPOLATE THAT RULE TO NEW WORK WITHOUT RE-MEASURING. It was true
	// when taken and is why this block exists, but it no longer predicts anything
	// here. Three attempts to buy isolate CPU with a smaller script all measured
	// ZERO: dropping 1,340,656 bytes of import-only wasm (-0.14ms, 95% CI
	// -0.72..+0.44), minifying 40% of the source (p90/p95 identical across
	// versions), and the floor itself — `GET /nope`, the router's 404, costs p50
	// 1ms against a 3.7MB script. Cloudflare compiles and snapshots the script at
	// DEPLOY, so a starting isolate does not re-parse it. What still costs is
	// STRUCTURE evaluated at module load, not bytes parsed: see
	// src/parser/tag-aliases.gen.ts, where 2,152 Map literals were 0.75ms an
	// isolate and the same data as a string is 0.16ms.
	//
	// html_handling "none" so nothing is rewritten under this Worker's own
	// paths, and not_found_handling "none" so anything that is not an asset
	// falls through to the router, which owns the 404.
	"assets": {
		"directory": "./public",
		"html_handling": "none",
		"not_found_handling": "none"
	},


	// Workers Cache: regionally tiered cache in front of the Worker, honoring
	// the Cache-Control headers the routes already mirror from upstream
	// (statics 30d/1h, pages+catalog 1h, /search 90s, /cards/* 16h). Hits skip
	// the Worker — and therefore cold isolate starts — entirely. no-store
	// responses (random_search, bootstrap page) are never cached, and neither
	// are the dispatch-level errors — `no-cache` on the 404/405/500/503
	// (src/routes/http.ts) and `no-store` on the per-address 429 — so the
	// honest-failure invariants hold. The exception is deliberate: /cards/*
	// sends its own tier on its own 400s and 404s, because Scryfall does — a
	// missing card is a stable fact.
	//
	// Per-version cache (default): each DEPLOY starts fresh, so statics can't go
	// stale across releases. A nightly IMPORT is not a deploy and so does not
	// reset anything, which is why it purges explicitly — see the coordinator's
	// `purge` phase (which runs once, right after notify) and
	// SylvanLibrarian.purgeCache. /search would have survived without one (90s,
	// and its stale-while-revalidate refreshes in the background), but /cards/*
	// holds Scryfall's 16 hours with no revalidation at all, so a card object
	// cached shortly before an import outlived the prices it was built from by
	// the better part of a day.
	"cache": { "enabled": true },

	// The engine store lives here, and KV is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for it: nothing
	// else holds an authoritative copy. The store is one complete rkyv archive PER
	// PARTITION, each published under its own immutable chunk family, plus one
	// routing filter and one manifest at `store:manifest` — written by the deploy
	// and the nightly cron, read by every SearchEngine DO.
	//
	// DO NOT WRITE THE PARTITION COUNT DOWN HERE. The builder derives it per build
	// from the corpus it just measured (TARGET_PARTITION_BYTES, src/import-publish.ts)
	// and every reader takes it from the manifest's `partition_count`; a literal in
	// this file could only ever be a stale copy of it. `bun scripts/store-age.ts`
	// reads the live shape. For scale only, and dated and attributed because it
	// rots: read 2026-08-16 off the all_cards corpus, with the code then standing
	// at content generation 32 / format 2026081616, the manifest was 10 partitions,
	// one chunk each, 412,869,984 raw archive bytes across the ten. Content
	// generation has moved twice since; do not treat those bytes as current.
	//
	// What that costs against the free tier (100k KV reads and 1k writes a day)
	// follows from the shape rather than from a fixed count. A publish writes every
	// partition's chunks plus the routing filter plus the manifest; a cold engine
	// object reads the chunks of ITS OWN partition and nothing else. So both totals
	// scale with `partition_count`, and neither is a number to write down here.
	//
	// HOW MANY PARTITIONS A REQUEST TOUCHES IS A ROUTING QUESTION, NOT A KV ONE, and
	// the two answers differ: a search reaches every partition (the isolate sends
	// ONE RPC to a gather object, which runs the two-phase gather across all N — see
	// PartitionedEngine and runTwoPhase), while an id-keyed lookup asks the routing
	// filter first and normally touches exactly one. Neither ever loads one large
	// archive. There is no separate card-object residue archive and no extra round
	// trip for `/cards/*`: generation 19 folded the residue into the printing
	// record, so `/search` and `/cards/*` read the identical bytes.
	//
	// Each engine DO ALSO keeps its partition in its own SQLite as a read-through
	// cache (src/engine/store-cache.ts), COMPRESSED chunk for chunk — decompressed
	// copies of every partition across every region do not fit the 5GB DO pool.
	// That is not a return to the design where DO storage WAS the store: nothing
	// there is authoritative, any miss or fault falls back to these keys, and it is
	// bounded by the nine regions rather than by every colo. It does consume the
	// pool, though, so size that from store-cache.ts's own accounting.
	//
	// Nothing to fill in by hand: the deploy creates the namespace if absent
	// and pins its id (scripts/align-kv-binding.ts) before wrangler reads this
	// file, which also repairs the binding after a namespace is deleted and
	// recreated.
	"kv_namespaces": [
		{
			"binding": "STORE_KV",
			"id": "0000000000000000000000000000pending"
		}
	],

	// ImportCoordinator is a plain SQLite-backed Durable Object: it serializes
	// import runs (only one at a time) and executes the whole import pipeline
	// itself via the wasm import module — Scryfall fetch, transform, tag
	// resolution, aggregation, store build, KV publish — in an alarm chain
	// that keeps each invocation far below the isolate CPU allowance. No
	// container, no external CI, no secrets.
	"durable_objects": {
		"bindings": [
			{ "name": "IMPORT_COORDINATOR", "class_name": "ImportCoordinator" },
			// The engine objects, one per region × replica × partition (engine-namespace.ts). The
			// colo-era objects of this namespace (engine-LAX…, before 2026-08-12) are released once
			// by the watchdog cron, gated by the RETIRED_ENGINE_SWEEP var below — see .env.example.
			{ "name": "SEARCH_ENGINE", "class_name": "SearchEngine" },
			// Per-IP token-bucket rate limiter (one tiny in-memory DO per IP);
			// limits are runtime env vars — see RATE_LIMIT_* in .env.example.
			{ "name": "RATE_LIMITER", "class_name": "RateLimiter" },
			// The nightly placement probe (backlog g1, src/engine/placement-probe.ts): created with a
			// location hint and a fresh unique id, it reports the colo it landed in and stores nothing,
			// so every probe object ceases to exist when it goes idle. 44 requests a night.
			{ "name": "PLACEMENT_PROBE", "class_name": "PlacementProbe" }
		]
	},
	"migrations": [
		{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["ImportCoordinator"] },
		{ "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SearchEngine"] },
		{ "tag": "v3", "new_sqlite_classes": ["RateLimiter"] },
		{ "tag": "v4", "new_sqlite_classes": ["PlacementProbe"] }
	],

	// The nightly rebuild, after Scryfall's daily bulk-data refresh window, and the import watchdog (src/import-watchdog.ts), which replaces a
	// coordinator the platform has wedged mid-run. Keep both strings in step with NIGHTLY_CRON and
	// WATCHDOG_CRON there — scheduled() routes on the exact string.
	"triggers": { "crons": ["17 11 * * *", "*/10 * * * *"] },

	// Plain vars, deployed with the code. Everything else this Worker reads at runtime is a Secret
	// (RATE_LIMIT_*, SHARDS_MAX, TRUSTED_API_KEYS — set per account, untouched by a deploy).
	// RETIRED_ENGINE_SWEEP drives c1's one-time colo-era sweep on the watchdog cron: "dry-run" logs
	// each object's size, "release" deletes the holders' storage, and the line goes once a release has
	// run (src/engine/retired-engine-sweep.ts, .env.example).
	"vars": { "RETIRED_ENGINE_SWEEP": "dry-run" },

	// The wasm engine is imported by the module name the code uses; the built
	// wasm-bindgen pkg is committed so Workers Builds needs no Rust toolchain.
	"alias": {
		"sylvan-engine-wasm": "./src/engine/wasm-shim.ts"
	},

	// Bundle the wasm engine + wasm import module and the vendored upstream
	// static assets.
	"rules": [
		{ "type": "CompiledWasm", "globs": ["**/*.wasm"], "fallthrough": true },
		{ "type": "Text", "globs": ["**/*.css", "**/*.html", "**/*.txt"], "fallthrough": true },
		{ "type": "Data", "globs": ["**/*.ico", "**/*.webp"], "fallthrough": true }
	]
}
